WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.680 --> 00:00:03.710
Surfers travel to the most remote corners of the world

00:00:03.710 --> 00:00:05.700
to find the perfect wave.

00:00:05.700 --> 00:00:07.319
However rare and fleeting--

00:00:07.319 --> 00:00:09.310
when conditions are just right,

00:00:09.310 --> 00:00:11.560
the results are magic.

00:00:13.450 --> 00:00:16.200
But these waves weren’t created by nature.

00:00:16.200 --> 00:00:19.560
They were made inside a wave pool in central California,

00:00:19.560 --> 00:00:23.520
nearly 200 kilometers from the Pacific Ocean.

00:00:25.240 --> 00:00:26.940
It’s the result of a collaboration

00:00:26.960 --> 00:00:29.920
between a fluid mechanics specialist, Adam Fincham,

00:00:30.000 --> 00:00:33.420
and 11-time world champion surfer, Kelly Slater.

00:00:34.530 --> 00:00:37.160
Wave pools for surfing date back more than fifty years

00:00:37.160 --> 00:00:39.760
but even the best have paled in comparison

00:00:39.940 --> 00:00:40.880
to ocean waves.

00:00:41.260 --> 00:00:44.380
Under lab conditions, a wave a few centimeters tall

00:00:44.380 --> 00:00:47.820
can be predictably modeled using linear equations.

00:00:48.670 --> 00:00:51.260
But scaling up, Fincham and his co-workers had

00:00:51.260 --> 00:00:53.160
many other factors to contend with--

00:00:53.440 --> 00:00:56.980
from turbulence to oscillations of the entire water body,

00:00:57.160 --> 00:00:59.040
which is called seiching.

00:00:59.320 --> 00:01:03.680
Scientist’s fine tuned wave shapes with Slater’s input,

00:01:03.680 --> 00:01:06.229
lab-built models, and supercomputers

00:01:06.229 --> 00:01:08.520
often running weeklong simulations.

00:01:08.940 --> 00:01:11.500
They applied their findings to an artificial lake

00:01:11.500 --> 00:01:14.610
originally made for water skiing that stretches

00:01:14.610 --> 00:01:17.440
700 meters long and 150 meters wide.

00:01:17.810 --> 00:01:21.060
A slab of carefully shaped metal called a hydrofoil

00:01:21.060 --> 00:01:22.660
is attached to a contraption that’s

00:01:22.720 --> 00:01:24.780
the size of a few train cars.

00:01:24.780 --> 00:01:27.560
And with the help of more than 150 truck tires,

00:01:27.740 --> 00:01:29.020
runs down a track.

00:01:29.320 --> 00:01:32.180
A cable pulls the hydrofoil at up to 30 kilometers

00:01:32.180 --> 00:01:34.240
per hour for the length of the pool,

00:01:34.620 --> 00:01:36.600
sculpting a wave that can stand

00:01:36.600 --> 00:01:38.700
more than 2 meters tall.

00:01:40.260 --> 00:01:42.300
The wave shape changes as &nbsp;it passes

00:01:42.480 --> 00:01:45.320
over contoured reefs along the bottom of the pool.

00:01:45.620 --> 00:01:48.780
Giant gutters serve as dampers to reduce seiching

00:01:48.780 --> 00:01:51.240
and limit bounce-back from the pool walls.

00:01:51.760 --> 00:01:54.400
Recently, some of the top professional surfers

00:01:54.540 --> 00:01:57.340
in the world were invited to the Surf Ranch to compete.

00:01:59.080 --> 00:02:02.240
Rides can last for 50 seconds and the wave alternates

00:02:02.240 --> 00:02:06.220
between faces to carve on and barreling sections.

00:02:11.120 --> 00:02:13.680
Stephanie Gilmore, six- time World Surf League

00:02:13.800 --> 00:02:15.860
champion, stayed in a barrel

00:02:15.860 --> 00:02:18.180
for an astonishing 14 seconds.

00:02:19.420 --> 00:02:20.900
The Surf Ranch is a prototype

00:02:20.900 --> 00:02:22.900
and is not open to the public.

00:02:23.080 --> 00:02:25.299
But its developers plan to build resorts

00:02:25.300 --> 00:02:27.940
around similar wave pools at landlocked locales.

00:02:28.650 --> 00:02:30.600
And the ability to adjust the wave could provide

00:02:30.609 --> 00:02:33.189
a controlled way for beginners to learn

00:02:33.189 --> 00:02:35.109
and for professionals to train.

00:02:35.109 --> 00:02:38.160
If these visions come true, Kelly’s wave

00:02:38.300 --> 00:02:40.080
could fundamentally alter the surf world

00:02:40.080 --> 00:02:42.740
and, artificial as it may be,

00:02:43.220 --> 00:02:46.600
redefine the search for the perfect wave.

